Predictive network management seeks to outrun the reactive horizon by forecasting traffic shifts before they manifest as congestion. We introduce Combo, a proactive sensing substrate that generates a Signal Availability Lead-Time (SALT) via aggregate elephant flow cardinality predictions. While our initial agenda aimed to reclaim the reactive blind spot, our empirical evaluation reveals the boundaries of this signal. Evaluation on backbone and datacenter traces shows that while Combo provides the momentum to tune adaptive thresholds and top-k rankings, its utility is limited by the network's reactive wall. We found that our proactive momentum degrades due to several barriers: aggregate cardinality signals obscure how much data flows actually carry, and our window-based forecasts lack ingress-port information, making them difficult to use for rerouting or path rebalancing decisions. Most importantly, we discovered a loopback effect in which the system's own success corrupts its future training data. This paper documents why aggregate EF cardinality is too coarse to close the loop on the control actions it motivates: knowing how many elephant flows are coming is not the same as knowing what to do about them.